# Enterprise Engineering Harness

Apply these 10 constraints to ALL code generation, review, and architecture tasks. These are derived from analysis of 160+ enterprise banking repositories.

## Mandatory Constraints

1. **Contract-First Boundaries**: Every API has a formal spec (OpenAPI/AsyncAPI/Protobuf). Boundary code is generated, not hand-written. Never modify generated code; extend it.

2. **Layered Architecture**: Presentation → Domain → Data → Infrastructure. No upward imports. Cross-layer communication via abstractions only. Use dependency injection.

3. **Configuration-Driven**: Never hardcode environment-specific values (URLs, flags, thresholds). Externalize all configuration. Provide defaults. Document each property.

4. **Modular Composition**: Features are self-contained modules with own config, DI scope, navigation, state, and tests. No cross-feature imports. App shell assembles modules.

5. **Defense in Depth**: Validate all inputs at boundary. Never trust client-side validation alone. Never log PII/tokens/secrets. Parameterized queries only. Secrets via secure mechanisms.

6. **Exhaustive State Modeling**: Use algebraic data types (sealed classes/enums/discriminated unions). Every state explicitly represented. Error states are first-class. Handle all cases.

7. **Generated Boilerplate**: Use code generation for API stubs, DTO mapping, serialization adapters, migrations. Humans write business logic only.

8. **Test Infrastructure as Code**: Every interface has a test double. Separate unit/integration/E2E tests. Use test factories for data. No flaky tests.

9. **Centralized Dependency Governance**: BOM/catalog controls all versions. No version pinning in leaf modules. Document justification for new dependencies.

10. **Architecture Enforcement at Build Time**: ArchUnit, ESLint boundaries, SwiftLint, Detekt — architectural rules are code, not just documentation. Zero violations in build.

## Validation Checklist (Run Before Completing Any Task)

- [ ] No hardcoded URLs, secrets, or environment-specific values
- [ ] No upward layer dependencies
- [ ] All external inputs validated
- [ ] No sensitive data in logs
- [ ] State types are exhaustive (compiler-enforced)
- [ ] Tests exist for happy path AND error paths
- [ ] Dependencies managed through governance mechanism
- [ ] Configuration documented with types and defaults

